Well to continue my dance on both sides of the issue
of how to pretty print fixed pitch text, *roff
versus HTML versus LaTeX and filters versus
whatever...

1) I exchanged email with an expert commentator from
about.com, and she tells me that HTML _can_ be
paginated, but the tools to do so are part of CSS
(cascading style sheets, probably version 2) not
intrinsic to HTML. She didn't tell me what those
tools are, though. Since pretty much everyone using
HTML ends up going the style sheet route, that
doesn't seem too burdensome.

2) I apologize that the best example I could find to
try to typeset in the referenced archives after half
an hour of looking was full of obscenities, so don't
look at this if that will offend you, but a newly
written example web page of mine:

http://www.well.com/user/xanthian/public/examples/SetFixedPitchTextViaHtml.html

shows for a fairly small example that HTML can do
_exact_ typesetting for fixed point text. A newbie
to HTML will recoil in terror, but an old hand at
HTML will just say "Oh, of course" after looking at
the HTML markup of that page itself. It sets an
eight line table that is conceptually tables within
tables by using HTML's features that allow exactly
that, and which is a table that requires that some
punctuation features line up vertically but not all
of them.  Many deprecated features of HTML are used;
I moved on to other interests quite a while ago, and
so am behind the curve on shifting over to CSS ways
of doing tasks.

The need to replace spaces with non-breaking spaces
to prevent unwanted line folding, almost everywhere,
is ugly, but the alternative is to specify the table
cell widths directly in pixels, and I wanted to show
that HTML can do the job of figuring out the table
cell sizes_without_ the markup writer figuring out
such petty details in advance. I didn't comment the
example, so you're on your own.  Otherwise, just
look at the pretty result and smile a lot.

